00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:11.930 align:start position:0%
the basic idea we're exploring is what  makes people advocate or share their  opinions with other people  and one fact that we examine in this  research is whether people believe that  other people's attitudes are fixed or  changing so some people believe that  your opinions are your opinions and  there's nothing you can do about them  like you may like sauerkraut or you may  hate it and there's nothing you can do  to change that and other people think  that attitudes are very variable and  they can change over time and so we  examined how these beliefs affect  people's likelihood of sharing their  opinions with others so as it turns out  each theory can have factors that make  you more likely to try to persuade other  people if I believe that attitudes are  stable over time I have greater  certainty about my own attitudes I think  my own attitudes will remain the same  over time and that makes me have more  conviction in those attitudes and when I  have more conviction in those attitudes  that makes me more likely to try to  persuade other people on the other hand  theories that attitudes are malleable  can also increase advocacy if I believe  that attitudes can change over time I'm  more likely to believe that your  attitudes can change and therefore I'm  more likely to try to persuade you  so if I know what type of theory you  hold if you have a theory that attitudes  are malleable for example and what I  want to do is promote that focus on  other people to focus on how other  people's attitudes can be changed and  that will increase your likelihood of  trying to persuade other people whereas  if I know that you hold an attitude of  stability then I want to direct your  focus to the stability of your own  attitudes which will give you greater  conviction in those attitudes and be  more likely to try to persuade other  people these days there's more and more  what marketers called media clutter lots  and lots of advertisers are clamoring  for the same limited bandwidth of  attention that consumers have and also  consumers are increasingly skeptical of  advertising efforts and so one  particularly effective form of  persuading other people is through  communications through trusted friends  and and loved ones  and so to the extent that we can get  people to share their opinions with  other people particularly for topics  that they may be reluctant to do so such  as political things we can increase the  likelihood that we will be able to bring  people around to to our desired point of  view
 

